WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.850
Driving through a building looks awesome.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.960
In reality, though, tankers
hate going through buildings.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.150
In one word, it's basements.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.680
Greetings, all. I'm Nicholas Moran.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.440
I commissioned as an
armor officer on M1 tanks

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.030
and then M3 Bradleys.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.520
These days, I work as a civilian historian

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.290
for the publisher of World of Tanks.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.420
Today, we're going to be
looking at tank battle scenes

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.370
in movies and television

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.520
and judging how real they are.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.400
This is absolutely correct.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.190
There's gun crews on
each side of the sponson.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.760
The tank is based off of a
Mark VIII "International,"

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.490
which was a very late World War I tank

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.520
built by both the UK and US.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.280
And at the time, British
tanks, the heavy tanks,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.870
had what were called sponson guns,

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.691
the turret that wasn't on the Mark VIII.

00:00:54.691 --> 00:00:58.560
[shouting instructions]

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.290
Shouting instructions to
each other wouldn't happen.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.490
It's too loud.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:03.960
Inside a World War I tank,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.840
the entire middle of that tank
was taken up by the engine.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.190
It was massive noise.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.960
What he's doing there,
he's using hand signals.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.430
That's the only way to communicate

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.872
inside a World War I tank.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.570
Firing a gun with anything
in the barrel is bad.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.520
You run the risk of
exactly what happened here.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.810
It's called petaling,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:29.160
when the gun barrel just
petals out like a flower.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.360
And your gun is done.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.327
Oftentimes, you'll see a tank will have

00:01:32.327 --> 00:01:35.034
a rubber muzzle cap on the end,

00:01:35.034 --> 00:01:38.807
purely to stop any dirt at
all from getting into it.

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:40.410
And it's designed that you can actually

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:41.644
shoot through the muzzle cap.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.377
To my knowledge, on-tank fighting

00:01:49.377 --> 00:01:51.510
has never been trained in any army.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.610
Personally, I'd have just
stuck my head out the top,

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.860
used a pistol. But,
again, not very dramatic,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.360
and it would kind of suck for Indy.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.790
I would have to give this scene maybe a 3

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.409
on feasibility grounds.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.090
A Tiger has appeared on the scene.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.120
Tiger had a mythical
status in World War II.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.570
It had the reputation because
it was very, very hard to kill

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.190
and because it had a heck of a gun.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.400
The 8.8 centimeter was lethal
to basically everything.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.710
The problem was that it looks
very much like a Panzer IV,

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:23.670
which was a lot more common.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.340
In actuality, historians
have found very few times

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.140
on the Western front
that American soldiers

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.850
ever actually met a Tiger.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.336
But for the purposes of
drama, a Tiger is fine.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:37.620
This was pretty realistic.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.770
Drivers are absolutely
blind inside their tanks,

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.155
and they're utterly reliant

00:02:42.155 --> 00:02:43.740
on instructions from the commander.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.990
So, that's how you would
get up close to a tank.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.880
Low, below the line of vision,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.015
below the level of depression

00:02:50.015 --> 00:02:51.315
that the guns can aim at you.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.850
We're going for the wheels.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.860
Because the Tiger was a breakthrough tank,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.840
it was heavily armored everywhere.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.940
And if you are infantry

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.740
and you don't have high-velocity cannons,

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.260
your best bet is to disable the tank

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.850
and get what's called a mobility kill

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:10.110
to immobilize the tank.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.535
Your two choices are going to be either

00:03:11.535 --> 00:03:14.220
the engine compartment
or the running gear.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.160
And the only thing that these sticky bombs

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.540
are going to have any particular effect on

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.380
will be the running gear.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.170
So, the sticky bomb, it was in the manual,

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.600
but it is also almost a
weapon of desperation.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.300
The military is supposed to
give you tools for the job.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.550
Late World War II era, you're
talking about the bazooka.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.440
Now, the problem is that
you only have so many rounds

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.370
of bazooka ammunition,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.920
and a bazooka is only so
effective against something

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.720
as heavily armored as a Tiger.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.293
So you're in desperation
mode to start with.

00:03:40.293 --> 00:03:42.247
Soldier: Both sides, get aboard!

00:03:42.247 --> 00:03:43.080
Get a grenade!

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.460
So, now we see one of the problems

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.350
with a tank going on its own.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.080
A tank shouldn't go on its
own for several reasons.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.497
But when you have infantry,

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:54.283
an infestation, we might
like to say as tankers,

00:03:54.283 --> 00:03:55.847
crawling on your tank, there's a process

00:03:55.847 --> 00:03:57.930
that's called "scratching
your friend's back."

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.190
What you do is you get on the radio,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:00.960
you call your buddy with a machine gun,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.120
and he will machine-gun your tank.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.680
Of course, the bullets aren't
going to affect your tank.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.389
You're in a tank. But it
will very much clean off

00:04:07.389 --> 00:04:10.313
or delouse the infantry that
are crawling on your tank.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.300
And that's your example of delousing.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.550
So, the 20-millimeter anti-aircraft gun,

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.740
like any anti-aircraft gun
with a high rate of fire,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.480
you aim that thing horizontally

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.340
and you will do a number on infantry.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.920
If there's any big myth about tanks,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.220
it's probably that they are
these invulnerable fortresses.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.990
In reality, they're not
invulnerable at all,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.550
and they're absolutely reliant

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.130
on the other parts of
the combined arms team.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.340
You have the desperation
of lightly armed infantry.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.960
You have this back-scratching.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.530
You have the immobilization
of a very dangerous tank.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.780
I mean, this is easily on the 9 scale.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.490
And that is absolutely accurate.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.860
The vast majority of
tanks that were destroyed

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.260
had no idea that the enemy was there.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:04:59.700
And, again, this is why you always

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.560
have to work in conjunction with infantry,

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.480
because they have more eyes than you do.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.160
They have better vision than you do.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.150
But more often than not,

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.970
the first indication
that somebody is there

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.380
is when you get shot at.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.780
And knocking off either
the first or the last tank

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.021
in an ambush in a row
like that is classic.

00:05:16.402 --> 00:05:18.727
What the f--- was it?

00:05:18.727 --> 00:05:20.880
That was an 88. It's a goddamn tank.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.860
With regard to the TCs in this case

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.720
keeping their heads out,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.460
there are two schools of thought.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.380
But certainly in the time
period of World War II

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.660
all the way through the
Cold War, even myself,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.850
it would be in a head-out position.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:37.020
The Western school of thought
is you keep your head out

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.780
because what you don't see will kill you.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:40.920
The other school of thought,

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.010
such as used by the Soviet
Army in the Cold War,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.350
was that you wanted to operate head-down.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.430
So you close the hatch,

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:48.900
no artillery could affect you,

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:51.300
no snipers could affect you.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.880
And the problem, of
course, is the reverse.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:57.390
You aren't able to see
whatever is about to kill you.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:00.570
So there is a balance to be struck.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:02.362
All right, all tanks, move forward.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.830
Wardaddy has decided for some reason

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:10.620
that he's going to charge the enemy tank.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.630
I'm not sure I would've
done quite the same thing,

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.332
because that simplifies
the problem for the enemy

00:06:16.332 --> 00:06:18.210
and just gives them
three targets in a line.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.570
If you can have what's
called a base of fire

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.310
and then a maneuver element,

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.050
then the enemy tank has a choice.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.620
It can engage the guy
that's just sitting there

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.570
pumping accurate shots at you,

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.600
or the German tank can
engage the flanking element,

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.160
who are coming around the side.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.230
And the 76-millimeter
gun on two of these tanks

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.721
actually would destroy a Tiger.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.420
OK, so, being obscured,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.390
the German tank has decided
to advance out of the smoke

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:49.650
as opposed to just sit there

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:52.200
and wait for whatever the
enemy were going to do.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.020
The Germans were very, very
big on individual initiative.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:58.260
The concept is called auftragstaktik.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.030
These days, we use a development of it

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:01.650
called mission command.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.980
But the bottom line is
you don't wait for orders.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:08.255
You take the initiative. You
make a decision on the spot.

00:07:08.255 --> 00:07:10.650
And that is exactly what
the German has done here.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.129
Wardaddy: Bible, put it up his
a-- while the armor's thin.

00:07:14.129 --> 00:07:15.577
I know it'll f---ing hit him!

00:07:15.577 --> 00:07:19.170
The reticle is accurate for
a World War II American tank,

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:22.020
and Shia, in this case, is
using the coaxial telescope.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.480
So you're going to see
graduations on the reticle.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.290
Each graduation is for a type
of round at a certain range.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:32.070
So if your target is,
let's say, 800 yards,

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:35.790
you will elevate the gun so
that the 800-yard graduation

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:36.960
is on the target.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.960
So, the single biggest cause
of missing in World War II

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:41.910
was range-estimation error.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:43.170
If you get the range wrong,

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.510
it doesn't matter if your aim is correct,

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.250
you will go over or short,

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.650
which is why we now have
modern fire-control systems

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:51.859
to do all that mathematics for you.

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:55.283
Again!

00:07:55.283 --> 00:07:56.200
On the way!

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.320
If you are in this sort of a knife fight,

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.030
which I wouldn't recommend getting into,

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:04.920
the first round set the engine alight,

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.210
but that doesn't necessarily say

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.220
that the tank crew in the turret

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:09.660
can't still get a shot off.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:12.240
They say you shoot at
a tank until it burns

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:13.710
or changes shape.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.290
So keep shooting until you
know that the threat is gone.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:18.570
This scene is an easy 9.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:22.320
What "Fury" lacks in perhaps
some of the technical accuracy,

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:24.720
it more than makes up for in understanding

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.962
the experience of being a tank crewman.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.730
Tanks don't go from 0
to 60 just like that.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:36.990
They're not sports cars.

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:39.240
But they look a lot faster
than they're actually going.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:40.200
By way of comparison,

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.960
the M1 will do 0 to 20 miles an hour

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:44.940
in somewhere over seven seconds.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.700
So to be launched like that,

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:51.990
you are relying a lot on the
momentum of the truck itself.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:53.910
But, hey, it's a 60-something-ton tank.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:55.426
It has a lot of momentum.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.130
This is obviously a derivative

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:03.900
of some sort of futuristic tank.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:07.140
In reality, it's a 1960s
Chieftain that they've dressed up.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.880
But one of the problems
you see with this tank

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.420
is you have the big glass
windows for the driver.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.350
In reality, you don't want
to have big glass windows

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.100
on a tank, because it's a weak spot.

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:18.959
Guys, we gotta come up with another plan.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:23.310
They got a tank.

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:25.560
If the opposition comes up with a tank

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.530
and you don't have anything
that's an antitank weapon,

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.300
yes, you are in a serious problem.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:33.000
All you can do is try
to be faster than it,

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.400
evade it, move to where it can't see you.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.467
If it can see you, it
can probably shoot you.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:44.160
The tank is not going to slow down

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.230
because of the massive recoil of the gun.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.810
Modern tanks have what's
called stabilization,

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:53.250
and what it's designed to do
is to keep the gun on target.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.050
So, with this in mind,

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:57.840
tanks are now very capable
of firing on the move.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.510
My initial thought is to
give it something like a 3,

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:01.680
because, yes, tanks are scary,

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:05.610
tanks will run over anything
that they come across,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:10.560
but the general concept
of this high-speed chase

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.209
is just not there.

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:21.180
So, that's Pierce sticking his head out

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:22.650
a little bit too much.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:23.970
If you're trying to drive a tank,

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:24.824
remember, you do have pedals,

00:10:24.824 --> 00:10:27.270
or usually the brake or the accelerator,

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.210
and you've got to be able to
be low enough to reach them.

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:33.300
So, a tank driver's seat has
two positions, up or down.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.370
So head out when you are not in combat,

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:37.380
when nobody's shooting at you.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:38.550
You can see a lot better.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:41.190
Because when you're driving
head-down with the hatch closed,

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:44.640
through a little periscope,
it's very easy to miss things,

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:47.280
and you run over people that
you don't want to run over.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:51.510
The very, very latest tanks do
have cameras rotated around,

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:53.747
but oftentimes they don't.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.350
Tanks are actually very,
very easy to drive.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.570
So, inherently, a tank is designed

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.150
to be as simple as possible to operate,

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:07.770
especially for a Soviet tank.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:10.253
If you can drive a car, you
can basically drive a tank.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.060
Some pretty cool drifting there.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.590
If you are going to drift in a tank,

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:19.620
you either want ice or
you want a Soviet track.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.210
So, there are two types of track

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:23.340
you can get on a tank, generally speaking:

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.220
rubber track or all-steel track.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:29.670
And Soviet tanks tended to
go with an all-steel system.

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:31.380
Although the metal grousers are great

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.930
for digging into dirt and mud

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:37.290
and getting you some excellent
cross-country traction,

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.230
on a road, there's very
little contact area

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:43.110
between the track and the tarmacadam.

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:45.540
So what you're doing
is you're just sliding,

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.220
quite literally sliding around.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:50.700
So that drifting was kind of fortunate

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:52.558
that they had a Soviet tank doing this.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.030
So, a tank driving through
a building looks awesome.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:04.410
In reality, though, tankers
hate going through buildings.

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:05.430
If there's any one reason

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:07.350
that tankers hate going through buildings,

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:09.450
in one word, it's basements.

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.970
Because if you think about it,
your typical house structure

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:13.500
is not designed to hold the weight

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:17.070
of a 40-something-ton
vehicle above a basement.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.560
And oftentimes, there is no indication

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:21.420
before you drive through the building

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.760
whether or not there
is a basement under you

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.080
or solid ground.

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:29.430
In this case, it would appear
that he has gotten lucky.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:33.783
I would probably put it
down more on the 5 area.

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:42.150
He is in a vehicle.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.400
All right, it's a black ...

00:12:45.637 --> 00:12:46.470
tank.

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:49.530
The definition of what is
a tank is somewhat debated.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.750
The traditional definition

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:54.240
often revolves around the
physical characteristics.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:55.200
It's going to have tracks,

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:56.250
it's going to have a gun,

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:57.330
it's going to have a turret,

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:58.674
and it's going to have armor.

00:12:58.674 --> 00:13:00.990
But that could also be used to describe

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:02.070
a self-propelled artillery piece.

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:04.740
It could be used to describe
an infantry fighting vehicle.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.380
And it cannot be used to
describe the very first tanks,

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:10.590
which had no turret, or the
S-tank, the Swedish tank,

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:12.270
which also had no turret.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:14.400
So, these days, you're probably better off

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.050
defining a tank by its role.

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:18.180
It's a way of getting a gun from A to B,

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:19.440
and it's very difficult to kill.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:23.240
So, the Tumbler here at
least has armor and mobility.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:31.980
Oh, it does have a gun, OK.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:33.600
I see absolutely no practical reason why

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:35.550
he needs to move to a
new driver's position

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:38.460
in order to operate this
feature of the vehicle.

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:40.680
Having the room inside this vehicle

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:43.710
to move from one position
to an entirely new position,

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:47.220
it's bad design, because you want to have,

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:49.470
that empty space needs
to be protected by armor.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:52.380
That larger amount of armor weighs more.

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:53.670
And that gives you flotation problems,

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:55.860
emphasizing the problem you
have now on a wheeled vehicle

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.170
of this extra weight coming
down on a set of wheels.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:00.720
Wheeled vehicles don't have
the advantage of the tracks.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.630
You spread the weight of a 60-odd-ton tank

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:07.138
onto a piece of dirt or mud
that it doesn't sink into it.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:12.870
We do have an impression perhaps

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:14.400
as to just how light this vehicle is,

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:16.650
because he is now driving on a roof.

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:19.890
Roofs are not usually designed
to hold armored vehicles

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:20.723
on top of them,

00:14:20.723 --> 00:14:22.650
which also means all this vehicle will do

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:24.180
is stop perhaps machine-gun rounds,

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:27.000
and it's not going to stop
a rocket-propelled grenade

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:29.350
or an antitank round. As
for the vehicle itself,

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:33.000
I don't think that there
is any actual equivalent

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:34.800
in military service around the world.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:35.640
The closest might be

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.170
for something like a
reconnaissance vehicle.

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:39.510
So, there is a vehicle called the Fennek,

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:42.510
and that is, in appearance, very similar.

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:44.400
It's sort of an angled four-by-four,

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.350
and it's designed to be fairly fast,

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:47.400
fairly low-profile,

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:50.310
to sneak around the battlefield
and do reconnaissance.

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.118
For what it is, I'll give it a 7.

00:14:57.099 --> 00:14:59.730
The tanks that they're using
are also very good vismods,

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:00.660
a visual modification.

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:02.880
What we have here is an earlier version

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:05.160
of today's American tank, the M1 Abrams,

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.140
and it's basically the
version that I was on

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:08.190
when I was in Iraq.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:10.740
For a moment, I was in
disbelief that they had used

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:14.550
actual M1s, because the
M1 has a jet engine.

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:17.086
It has an extremely distinctive sound.

00:15:17.086 --> 00:15:19.919
[engine whirring]

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:24.295
And in this movie, you
hear the engine starting,

00:15:24.295 --> 00:15:27.720
and the sound designers
put in the turbine engine,

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:32.340
and that was enough pause that
you realize, are they Abrams?

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:35.370
So what they've done is they've
taken British Centurions,

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:37.353
they've made it into an M1.

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:41.820
So, real tanks don't drive that close.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:44.610
You're going to have 100 or
200 yards in between tanks,

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:46.023
because if you're that
close, you make yourself

00:15:46.023 --> 00:15:48.668
a perfect target for artillery or aircraft

00:15:48.668 --> 00:15:51.110
or anything else with a blast radius.

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:53.250
Why give the enemy an easy shot?

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:55.380
The idea of this battle happening at night

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:58.500
is absolutely in line with
American doctrine of the time.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:03.330
Set in the 1991 Gulf War,
commonly known as Desert Storm.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:06.120
The US in particular owned the night.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:07.950
With modern night-vision gear,

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:10.170
it cannot be understated the advantage

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.630
that these pieces of equipment
would give over their enemy,

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:14.580
who could not see or fight at night.

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:16.680
The most famous of the
'91 battles like this

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:18.300
was called 73 Easting.

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.150
A swarm of tanks coming at you,

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:22.425
firing on the move, at night.

00:16:22.425 --> 00:16:23.490
You can't see them.

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.680
All you know is that there is explosions

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:27.660
and your own tanks are blowing up.

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:31.800
And it was absolute textbook shock attack,

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.720
and it has become one of
the most famous battles

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.079
in US Armor Branch history ever since.

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.229
So, what he's holding there is,

00:16:42.229 --> 00:16:43.754
I believe it's a PVS-7.

00:16:43.754 --> 00:16:46.740
It's an earlier version
of an image intensifier

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:48.210
that we'd use even today.

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:49.560
On these earlier M1 tanks,

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:50.820
the commander's handheld vision

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:53.297
is not as good as a thermal imager.

00:16:54.197 --> 00:16:55.287
All right, baby.

00:16:55.287 --> 00:16:56.770
Hold on, son. They got no range.

00:16:56.770 --> 00:16:57.930
The commander has dropped down.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:16:59.160
He's now looking through what's called

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:01.500
the gunner's primary sight extension,

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:02.850
and this allows him to see

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:05.452
exactly what the gunner is looking at.

00:17:05.452 --> 00:17:08.167
Calm down. Calm down, son.
Find me another target.

00:17:08.167 --> 00:17:09.000
Loaded.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:10.140
- Identify.
- Up!

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:11.910
- Fire!
- On the way.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:14.040
So, the coordination in here
is perfectly reasonable.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.960
The crew of the M1 is the driver, loader,

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:17.310
gunner, and commander.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:19.710
The commander confirms the target.

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:21.060
He gives the fire command.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:25.080
The loader loads the round,
arms the gun, yells "up."

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:26.670
He keeps back out of the way

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:28.440
because he doesn't want
to get hit by that 120

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:29.798
as it comes back to him.

00:17:30.899 --> 00:17:33.000
Cease fire, cease fire.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:35.027
It's absolutely reasonable

00:17:35.027 --> 00:17:38.580
that there would've been
personnel outside of the hatches

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:41.370
shooting machine guns if
they had to get that close.

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:43.050
Now, ordinarily, you wouldn't
want to get that close.

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:45.840
You'd actually stop, and
you will let the infantry

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:47.580
that you were supposed
to be operating with,

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:48.900
the last 100 yards,

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:51.427
while the tanks provide
the overwatching support.

00:17:51.427 --> 00:17:52.517
Fire!

00:17:54.252 --> 00:17:55.523
You just lit up a friendly.

00:17:55.523 --> 00:17:57.720
I heard you. We -- what?

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:58.553
Oh, my God.

00:17:58.553 --> 00:18:00.662
You just lit up Boylar, godd--- it!

00:18:00.662 --> 00:18:03.540
So, he has just fired and
engaged a friendly tank.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:06.630
It's called, in army
speak, a blue on blue.

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:08.160
Now, friendly fire has been a problem

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:10.800
since day one of ranged combat.

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.500
The problem that you had with the M1,

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:15.420
especially with the early thermal imagers,

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:18.780
was that it was much, much
better at spotting targets

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:20.550
than it was at identifying them.

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:23.940
And at a long range or
if surrounded by fire,

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:26.070
such as it is here,

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:28.440
you didn't always know for sure

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:29.760
what it was that you were engaging.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:31.350
And the other problem
is that friendly fire

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:34.350
has become a lot more of a thing.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:38.220
Because recent combat
has been so lopsided,

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:42.120
that if the enemy kills one
of your guys, you know it.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:44.130
They will have to live with the effects

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:46.020
of they have just killed their friends,

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.000
which is exactly the
premise of the movie here.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:49.470
So, I'd have to give them a 9.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.240
Overall, the tank battle scene

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:53.313
in "Courage Under Fire" are excellent.

00:18:55.110 --> 00:18:55.943
Let's go.

00:18:55.943 --> 00:18:57.491
All right, move on.

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:01.920
Every tanker I know wants
to be Oddball secretly,

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:03.270
and I'm guilty of that too.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:09.120
We tried hooking up a
loudspeaker to our tank once

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.154
to play music. It didn't
work out, unfortunately.

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:13.647
Oddball: Fire.

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:17.010
OK, so Oddball has fired a paint round.

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:19.590
Such things do not exist,
but Oddball is Oddball.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:20.790
What he has done, though,

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:23.490
given the situation that
Kelly and Oddball were in,

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:25.200
that's pretty much how I'd do it.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:27.330
If you're going to shoot at a Tiger,

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:29.064
the rear is the place
you're going to do it.

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.810
If I was the German tanker,

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:39.000
I probably would not
have gone down this road

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:40.080
in the first place.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:42.720
Tanks rule in open area like deserts.

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:47.130
In urban areas and in dense
forests, infantry rule,

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:49.470
and this is part of the reason why.

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:52.170
Since you can't see the
corners like you can in a car,

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:53.910
you are reliant on somebody on the outside

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:55.682
telling you which way to go forwards,

00:19:55.682 --> 00:19:56.873
backwards, left, or right.

00:19:56.873 --> 00:19:59.640
This is a sort of situation
where you need infantry.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:01.020
Subject to minor details,

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:02.940
like a tank which fires paint rounds,

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:05.370
has a large loudspeaker on the side

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:07.590
to play morale-boosting music,

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:09.300
that's actually a pretty good scene.

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.760
So I'd give that maybe an 8.

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:13.740
Another mandatory movie for tankers.

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:16.210
My favorite tank movie? Well ...

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:18.270
very, very difficult, because
it's a three-way fight.

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:21.300
Two of them we've seen here:
"Fury" and "Kelly's Heroes."

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:24.000
The third one that I'd
call out is "The Beast,"

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:25.980
which is from the 1980s.

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:30.000
It's about a Soviet tank
crew lost in Afghanistan.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:30.990
Thanks for watching.

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:33.693
If you enjoyed this, why not
click on the video above?

